Reason:
The line unconditionally writes to index `ts->ts_final_idx + 1` of the array `info->status.rates`. The array has size `IEEE80211_TX_MAX_RATES` (which is 4), meaning valid indices are 0–3. When `ts->ts_final_idx` equals 3, the index becomes 4, which is out of bounds. This causes an array-index-out-of-bounds error.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (ts->ts_final_idx + 1 < IEEE80211_TX_MAX_RATES)
    info->status.rates[ts->ts_final_idx + 1].idx = -1;
Reason:
The fix adds a bounds check before the assignment. It only writes the sentinel `-1` if `ts->ts_final_idx + 1` is strictly less than `IEEE80211_TX_MAX_RATES`, ensuring the index is within the valid range of the array (0–3). This prevents the out-of-bounds access.

